  

<h2 id="what-two-people-selling-something-taught-me-about-generosity-and-productivity">What two people selling something taught me about generosity <em>and</em> productivity</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/df30d7f5-96fe-4bb6-bd51-6bd14fe5e4ac/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260808201628.png" alt="Photo of one pair of hands giving a small ball to another pair of hands, overlaid at the top with many yellow orange bokeh-style circles of light" width="2292" height="1524"></p>
<p>Photo of hands by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/hands-passing-the-globe-to-the-person-8542538/" target="_blank">Mikhail Nilov</a>, photo of background lights by <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/vibrant-defocused-lights-as-background-4279017/" target="_blank">Ave Calvar Martinez</a> — images altered and combined by me</p>
<hr>
<p>I’ve just read two truly useful articles, pointing to products I have no intention of using. I’m nodding my head at the generosity. This is the way to go about hawking what you’re selling! And it applies even when you’re not selling anything.</p>
<h2 id="the-articles">The articles</h2>
<p>1 - <a href="https://www.notedexapp.com/blog/your-system-should-fit-on-one-card" target="_blank">Your system should fit on one card</a><br>
How to calmly focus on the next task, without losing sight of where it fits into the bigger picture.</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>A simple system is hard to abandon<br>
</li>
<li>Friction isn’t neutral<br>
</li>
<li>The list is the interface. The interface is the list.<br>
</li>
<li>Pick the project you’re most behind on. Open a blank note — paper, digital, doesn’t matter. Write down, in plain words, the four fields. Project name. The goal. The next action. The deadline, if any. That’s your card. Keep it visible. When you’ve done the action, erase it and write the next one. That’s the entire workflow.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>2 - <a href="https://blog.maxclax.com/notion-obsidian-omnifocus-and-paper-10-years-of-trying-everything-heres-what-survived-0ba521c6ec90" target="_blank">10 years of trying everything, what survived fits on one page</a></p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>What survived GTD:&nbsp;the Mind Sweep, the Inbox, and the Someday/Maybe list<br>
</li>
<li>What survived Agile Results:&nbsp;the Friday Reflection, three outcomes at every scope, and Hot Spots<br>
</li>
<li>What survived the 12 Week Year:&nbsp;the quarter as a sprint<br>
</li>
<li>You don’t need a system. You need the handful of moves that still work when you’re at your lowest capacity, because those are the only ones that will ever be there when it counts.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="the-take-aways">The take-aways</h3>
<p>The first thing I took away from these two articles is that of tailoring the simplicity of your system to the end you are trying to attain. Or, do what works and drop the rest.</p>
<p>The second take away is that no matter what you’re selling, it’s good to put something of value out there. This is not about give-me-your-email-for-a-lead-magnet content marketing. It’s about an abundance mindset with no thought of <em>any</em> type of reward.</p>
<h3 id="the-invitation">The invitation</h3>
<p>I will leave you today with an invitation to consider the opportunities for generosity that lie before you. Whether or not you have an item for sale isn’t the point.</p>
<p>This invitation is about putting something out there to improve someone’s life (hopefully multiple someones, but one is an excellent start), no strings attached.</p>
<p>You might —</p>
<ul>
<li>Write in public about something you wish you’d known, that you now know, to help people avoid whatever trap you fell into the first time<br>
</li>
<li>Share a great deal you’ve found, without an affiliate link<br>
</li>
<li>Tell someone whose job it is to help you (shop assistant, tour guide, nurse) they did well and made a good difference in your day. Be specific, be sincere.<br>
</li>
<li>Follow your heart, it will point you to where your specific skills might be needed.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="the-inspiration">The inspiration</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>They might not need me, but they might.<br>
I’ll let my head be just in sight;<br>
a smile as small as mine might be<br>
precisely their necessity.<br>
—Emily Dickinson, 1897</p>
</blockquote>
